The fog didn’t roll in; it slammed against the glass of the Lantern Room like a wet, gray fist. Elias wiped his hands on a rag that had seen better decades, the smell of kerosene and old salt clinging to his skin. He was seventy-two, and the lighthouse was the only thing in the world that still made sense to him. The sea was chaos, but the light was order.

He was polishing the Fresnel lens, a ritual as sacred as prayer, when the brass speaking tube in the corner gave a violent shudder.

Elias froze. The tube connected the Lantern Room to the keeper’s cottage below, a hollow pipe that hadn’t carried a voice in three years, not since Martha died. He stared at the mouthpiece, his heart hammering a frantic rhythm against his ribs.

*Click.*

A sound like a coin dropping into a slot. Then, a voice. Not the static hiss of wind, but a clear, terrified whisper.

"Elias. Don’t light it."

Elias dropped the polishing cloth. He stepped toward the tube, his boots heavy on the iron grating. He pressed his ear to the cold brass. "Who is this?"

"It’s me," the voice said
